From: Macron <noreply@github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dxmt: start a frame only when the encoder is about to be free
 for it (dxmt.latencySleep)

A frame passes three stages, each on its own thread: the application until
Present, DXMT's encode thread until the frame's last command buffer is
committed, and the GPU. The encode thread takes one frame at a time, and a
present holds it until a drawable is free. An application that it holds back
runs as far ahead as the frame latency limit allows: with DXGI's default of
3, three frames wait between the application's input and the screen. On an
M3, a program drawing 6,000 small quads a frame in a window ran at 120 frames
a second with 3.0 frames queued and 33 ms from a frame's start to the end of
its GPU work. On Windows the GPU vendors' low-latency modes (NVIDIA Reflex,
AMD Anti-Lag 2) take such a queue away for applications that use them; DXMT
answers neither.

FrameLatencyTracker (dxmt_latency.cpp) follows each frame through the three
stages. When at least three of the last seven frames waited for the encoder,
Present sleeps until the next frame, started then, reaches the encoder just
as it is done with the frames in flight, less dxmt.latencyTolerance. It takes
the longest time the application needed in those seven frames and the
shortest the encoder needed, so that a wrong estimate starts a frame early (a
moment in the queue) and not late (the encoder without work). The same
program then ran at 120 frames a second with 1.0 frames queued and 16.6 ms
(eight runs each way on the M3, tools/perf/latency.sh in Macron's
repository). DXVK's latency tracker (dxvk_latency_builtin.cpp) does this for
the GPU.

For the GPU it is not done here. Two readings of when the GPU takes a frame
can be tried with dxmt.latencyGpuModel, and neither is on:
- "finish" takes the GPU to work on one frame at a time, as DXVK's does. On
  the M3, with the GPU as the limit, Metal reported 33 ms of work on each
  frame and frames started 20 ms apart, and with this reading the program
  fell from 46 to 51 frames a second to 20 to 33 while the Mac was cool
  (its queue did fall, from 3.0 frames to 1.2 to 1.4).
- "start" asks Metal when the GPU started the frame's first command buffer
  (GPUStartTime) and takes the next one to be due one interval later. For
  that program Metal reported no wait before the start however many frames
  were queued, so this reading never acted.

- dxmt.latencySleep (default True): the sleep.
- dxmt.latencySleepWithVsync (default True): whether it also applies to a
  present that waits for the display (vsync, a frame cap). Such a present
  holds the encode thread until a drawable is free, so the encoder's time is
  then the display's interval. False leaves those presents to the frame
  latency limit alone.
- dxmt.latencyTolerance (microseconds, default 1000, 0 to 50000): how much
  earlier than computed a frame starts.
- dxmt.latencyGpuModel (default none; finish, start): see above.
- dxmt.logLatency (default False): every 300 frames, one info line with the
  frame rate, the time from a frame's start to the end of its GPU work (median
  and maximum of the last frames), the frames in flight, the sleep, what the
  application and the encoder took and how long frames waited for the
  encoder, and Metal's times for the GPU.
- dxmt.logLatencyFrames (default False): one info line a frame with all of
  its times, for working out what a change to the above would do.
- The sleep waits on an address nobody changes (RtlWaitOnAddress, which
  counts in 100 ns) and spins the last 300 microseconds. Sleep() counts in
  milliseconds, and it and NtDelayExecution yield the processor before they
  wait under Wine; on a busy three-core machine that yield cost whole time
  slices and the program two thirds of its frame rate.
- One info line says the frame latency limit in use when a device is made,
  and one each time the application asks for another. (Deadlock asks for 1.)

